  <nav class="qa-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <ol>
      {breadcrumbs.map((b, i) => (
        <li>
          {i < breadcrumbs.length - 1
            ? <><a href={b.url}>{b.name}</a><span aria-hidden="true"> › </span></>
            : <span aria-current="page">{b.name}</span>}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <header class="qa-header">
    <p class="qa-pillar">Myth · MYTH·17</p>
    <h1>Myth: Ravens Always Travel in Pairs</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <div class="id-verdict verdict-partly-true">Verdict: Partly True</div>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Established breeding pairs do stay together year-round on their territory and are frequently seen as a pair. But a substantial portion of the raven population — non-breeding young birds aged 1–3 years — lives in nomadic flocks of dozens to hundreds. Ravens also gather in large communal roosts. The "ravens travel in pairs" observation is accurate for territorial breeding adults, not for the broader population.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Breeding Pair</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Established raven pairs do maintain close association year-round on their breeding territory. Outside the breeding season they forage together, roost together (or at an adjacent site), and defend the territory jointly. For observers in areas with breeding territorial ravens — upland or wilderness environments — the pair is the most visible social unit, reinforcing the "ravens in pairs" image.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Non-Breeding Floater Population</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> A large fraction of any raven population consists of non-breeding "floaters" — young birds (1–3 years) and failed or unmated adults that have not established breeding territories. These birds do not live as pairs; they live in loose nomadic flocks, travelling together across wide areas in search of food.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>Heinrich's field studies showed that floater groups could number dozens of birds at a food source, with the birds moving as a loose group between food discoveries and roosting communally at night. Floater ravens outnumber breeding pairs in most populations during winter months, so at any given moment, more ravens may be seen in groups than in pairs.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Communal Roosts</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens communally roost — groups of birds congregating at a shared sleeping site — particularly in winter. Roosts documented in North America and Europe have ranged from dozens to several hundred birds. The roosting birds are predominantly non-breeding floaters, but territorial birds may also join roosts outside the breeding season.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>The function of communal roosting includes information sharing (birds that failed to find food can follow successful foragers from the roost the next morning — the "information centre hypothesis"), predator protection through many-eyes vigilance, and possibly social assessment of potential mates.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>What We Actually See</h2>
    <p>The mythology of "ravens in pairs" is reinforced by:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Norse symbolism: Huginn and Muninn are a pair.</li>
      <li>Heraldry and folklore: ravens in pairs are a common artistic motif.</li>
      <li>Observations of territorial breeding birds, which are the most visible raven individuals near human settlement in many northern environments.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>All of these represent genuine aspects of raven social life, but they create an incomplete picture. Ravens are both pair-bonded (as breeding adults) and highly social in groups (as non-breeding birds).</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Related Pages</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/behaviour/raven-social-life-and-roosts/">Raven social life and roosts</a></li>
      <li><a href="/behaviour/raven-juveniles-and-dispersal/">Raven juveniles and dispersal</a></li>
      <li><a href="/questions/what-is-a-group-of-ravens-called/">What is a group of ravens called?</a></li>
      <li><a href="/questions/are-ravens-monogamous/">Are ravens monogamous?</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-faq qa-section" aria-labelledby="faq-h2">
